    "body": "Cadillac brought its Formula 1 team racecar to the 2026 AUSA conference in Washington. (Breaking Defense)\nWASHINGTON — The Association of the United States Army conference continued today in downtown DC. Drones and counter-drone systems were abundant on the show floor this year, including one amphibious robot designed to carry other ground robots across the water. Catch all that and more in these snapshots from the expo down below.\nAnd don’t miss our expansive coverage of the 2026 trade show right here .\nAUSA mascots Sgt. Hero and George Washington descend the escalator at the 2026 annual conference. (Aaron Mehta / Breaking Defense)\nParker Hannifin showcased many of its components onboard the MDH Strategic Services Airwolf c-UAS system, which is shown here fixed atop the RCR 6WAP vehicle. (Breaking Defense)\nGeneral Atomics Aeronautical Systems Inc. and Hanwha Aerospace co-developed the Mojave short takeoff and landing (STOL) uncrewed aircraft, pictured here at the 2026 AUSA trade show. (Breaking Defense)\nHanwha Defense USA displayed a range of scale model armor and artillery systems on the AUSA show floor. (Breaking Defense)\nBAE’s Multi-Domain Artillery Cannon System stands tall at the 2026 AUSA trade show in Washington, DC. (Breaking Defense)\nThe Ukrainian military’s 3rd Army Corps brought a variety of small drones to the 2026 AUSA trade show. (Valerie Insinna / Breaking Defense)\nKyiv-based defense tech firm Stellarion brought several fiber-optic drones to the 2026 AUSA expo. (Valerie Insinna / Breaking Defense)\nUkrainian defense firm Tencore showcased an amphibious unmanned ground vehicle called MAT1LDA (Mesa Associates’ Tactical Integrated Light-Force Deployment Assembly), which is carrying Tencore’s TerMIT ground robot, at the 2026 AUSA trade show. (Valerie Insinna / Breaking Defense)\nTeledyne Qioptiq brought several thermal weapons sights including the Phoenix-XR (right) and the Dragon-C12 S (middle) to the 2026 AUSA conference. (Breaking Defense)\nIsrael Aerospace Industries’ and Sig Sauer’s Fire Storm 250 armed tactical quadcopter hangs above attendees of the conference. (Breaking Defense)\nOverlandAI’s ULTRA autonomous ground system hosts a multi-mission hemispheric radar from Leonardo DRS, shown here at the 2026 AUSA expo. (Breaking Defense)\nLeonardo DRS displays its Extended Mast Surveillance System, which features an exMHR radar system on a trailer platform with an extendable mast for a higher-altitude perspective. (Breaking Defense)\nPHOTOS: AUSA 2026\nGallery\nPresented by\nPHOTOS: AUSA 2026\n×\n1.\nAttendees file in during the first day of the 2026 Association of the US Army annual conference in Washington, DC, on October 12, 2026. (Breaking Defense)\n2.\nPatriot, who is expected to go to a wounded veteran when the dog’s training finishes, is happy to greet attendees outside the Shield AI booth on the show floor. (Breaking Defense)\n3.\nA close-up look at a munition from Sierra Nevada's Brawlr (Battery Revolving Advanced Weapons Launcher Reconfigurable) air-defense system, as seen at the 2026 AUSA conference. (Breaking Defense)\n4.\nA wider shot of Sierra Nevada's Brawlr air-defense system at the 2026 AUSA conference. (Breaking Defense)\n5.\nOshkosh's electric Joint Light Tactical Vehicle on the AUSA show floor Oct. 12, 2026. (Breaking Defense)\n6.\nHanwha Defense USA showed off its 155 mm artillery shells at the 2026 AUSA expo. (Breaking Defense)\n7.\nAEO Night Hawk Systems displayed their surveillance assembly, with sensors honing in on bystanders at the 2026 AUSA conference in Washington. (Breaking Defense)\n8.\nAeroVironment’s VAPOR is an all-electric, single-rotor vertical takeoff and landing (VTOL) helicopter unmanned aircraft system. Here it is suspended above attendees at the 2026 AUSA conference. (Breaking Defense)\n9.\nGoodyear representatives brought a sturdy but very unusual \"moon tire\" for lunar exploration to the 2026 AUSA expo. (Breaking Defense)\n10.\nColt optics had one of the busiest displays, with dozens of attendees observing a wall of assault rifles with specialized optics at the 2026 AUSA expo. (Breaking Defense)\n1 /10\nPresented by",
